landing-review: landing 11-5b (tagpu_native.c's GL bring-up), level high, 2026-09-19

Range main...HEAD at ad9b605..d68ee7e, main at ad9b605; a dedicated read-only
reviewer with the mingw toolchain and objdump, 77 tool uses. Raised to high
because the landing changes a PUBLISHED value that controls what the engine
draws. NO behavioural defect. Three MEDIUM, two LOW groups, two SPECULATIVE.
All verified against the source before acting.

What the reviewer proved rather than accepted: the ordering (s_ownWin set once
at render_vk.c:145, cleared nowhere, all ten references inspected;
tagpu_overlay_draw has one call site; tagpu_native_frame has one caller); that
removing the gl_draws term is inert in every reachable state it was asked to
consider -- first frame, glreset, teardown, steady state; that
tagpu_owndraw.c:284's `g_ssSkip = (g_ssTerr || g_ssPass) && g_sshadow` makes
g_ssPass permanently 0, so the skip is driven entirely by terrown's key-fill
today, which is the pre-landing behaviour exactly; that the de-indented
hand-over is byte-identical to main's with four spaces removed (65 lines,
diff -u empty); that all six shader literals are byte-identical AFTER FULL
PREPROCESSING, which is what spirv-gen.py reads; and the pragma bracket, by
re-running the probe.

M1 -- the pre-existing paragraph twelve lines ABOVE the gate still said
"`gl_draws` is ANDed in ... without it a GL -> Vulkan switch would carry one
raised frame across the seam", contradicting the new paragraph below it on the
one expression that decides whether the engine draws structure shadows. A
later author adding a Vulkan painter could have restored a lane term and
re-pinned the gate at 0 -- doubled shadows, silent. Rewritten. Two stale counts
in the same block fixed: seven SSHADOW_NONE returns, not nine, and the
hand-over return it cited is gone.

M2 -- my comment said `pal` is "read for the log line and for that reason
only". There is no such log line; `pal` is the argument to
tagpu_r3d_atlas_frame(), which steps the 3DO atlas's Classic++ restore. A
sweep trusting it would have deleted a live call.

M3 -- s_fogTex/s_fogLutTex were kept because tagpu_hires_draw.c reads
v->fogTex. It does, but those are TAGPU_HVIEW fields -- a DIFFERENT STRUCT.
TAGPU_FXVIEW.fogTex/.fogLut had no reader anywhere. Both deleted with the
write that was their reason to stay; the struct fields keep a tombstone.
Second instance in one landing of "it is published" being inferred from a
name: s_palTex was the first and the compiler caught it, this one survived the
compiler because only the READ was missing. Rule recorded: trace the field,
not the word. It also retires a false claim of dependency on escalation
reason 1.

LOW -- four counts in gpu-status §2.70, each a claim of verification: 27 GL
entry points not 29 (the breakdown summed to 34 against its own total of 32,
the same double-count the 11-5a review caught in §2.69); 21 of the 24
SURVIVING functions, not of 28; fourteen GL object names, not seven; and "each
of those three published outside the gate" is false of the palette, whose
block was GL end to end -- contradicting this landing's own code comment. Four
stale comments fixed, plus one sentence of mine that over-reached about
"every other return".

SPECULATIVE, both pre-existing and both filed for 11-5e rather than acted on:
s_fogLut is write-only and has been since 11-3 -- proved by the optimiser
eliding both stores in BOTH revisions, which it can only do with no reader in
the TU; and tagpu_owndraw.c:1016's g_ssPass watchdog is inert for the same
reason the gate is, and becomes live again the moment a Vulkan painter sets
s_ssSuppress, which is the right shape.

The fix commit's own code delta, measured: 23 of 24 functions instruction-
identical, tagpu_native_frame minus exactly three instructions (the two dead
stores).

Fixes: b5cdfe1.
